1
00:00:03,401 --> 00:00:07,255
Machine & tools holds the tool table: every cutter and drill on the N-200, by number.

2
00:00:07,255 --> 00:00:11,679
It ships as placeholder data, because only your shop knows its real tools. The amber box counts what is left to set.

3
00:00:11,679 --> 00:00:16,952
Each row is one tool: number, type, diameter, max depth. Programs call tools by these numbers.

4
00:00:16,952 --> 00:00:21,173
Tool sizes stay in millimetres even in an inch shop: bits are bought metric, and a drill must match exactly.

5
00:00:21,173 --> 00:00:26,323
T205, a 6 mm drill for the TANDEM runner holes, is in the table from the start, so slide holes always find a drill.

6
00:00:26,323 --> 00:00:30,742
Edit opens the tool. Measure the real bit before you change anything: the program trusts these numbers.

7
00:00:30,742 --> 00:00:36,779
Mark as confirmed only once the number, diameter and depth match the bit in the machine. That clears its badge.

8
00:00:36,779 --> 00:00:43,109
How holes find a drill: by diameter, within the drill matching tolerance (0.1 mm). An 8 mm hole uses T203, never a 7 mm bit.

9
00:00:43,109 --> 00:00:48,404
The cut-out tool is chosen here too: T101, the 12 mm compression cutter, sets the spacing nesting leaves between parts.

10
00:00:48,404 --> 00:00:53,467
When the real N-200 table is ready, Import tools loads it in one go; then the placeholder switch can go off.

11
00:00:53,467 --> 00:00:56,668
Until then every job’s export check lists these tools to configure, and nothing should be cut.
